I. SCORPION PROTOTYPE DEVELOPMENT AND DOCUMENTATION
During Senior Design I, the Scorpion team developed three versions of a mobile robot with a motorized base, a pulley-driven intake, and a tail assembly. The first prototype demonstrated that an operator could drive the robot from a laptop. By the third version (V3), the team had assembled the chassis, mounted the electronics, and operated the intake. Testing also revealed three mechanical issues that guided the next design revisions: the intake shafts slipped out of their mounts, the chassis caught on the hoop during a driving test, and two tail gears did not mesh smoothly.
Scorpion is a mobile robot developed at the University of Central Arkansas for Senior Design I. Its scorpion-shaped body combines a 3D-printed chassis, a motorized drive system, a pulley-driven intake, and a raised tail with a gear assembly. An operator drives the robot from a laptop using keyboard commands sent to the onboard Raspberry Pi. Over three prototype versions, the team revised the chassis to fit the electronics and moving parts. Testing demonstrated that the robot could drive and operate its intake. The tail gears were still undergoing fit checks at the time of the latest weekly report. 
A. Chassis and Drive System
The first prototype provided a platform for testing the drive system before integrating the intake and tail. Alex designed and printed the base, and Mohamed Lamizana designed the motor housing in FreeCAD and helped assemble the prototype. Azat Kossanov connected a voltage regulator to the Raspberry Pi and motor driver, wrote scripts for motor testing and keyboard control, and configured a Secure Shell (SSH) connection between the laptop and the Pi. With the electronics mounted on the base, the team successfully drove the prototype using the laptop keyboard.
For V3, the team printed the chassis in two large halves. Alex revised the mounting holes and reinforced the center connection between the intake tunnel and the electronics compartment. The team then installed the revised motor holder and positioned the electronics to leave clearance for the gear system. During the first V3 driving test, the assembled robot moved under its own power but caught on the hoop. This trial identified a clearance issue that had not been apparent during assembly. The team proposed adjusting the hoop and repeating the driving test.
B. Pulley and Intake Development
The early pulley assembly used rubber bands to transfer motion. During assembly, the team found that the 5-mm bands were too large for the pulley arrangement and switched to 3-mm bands. The team subsequently tested the pulley mechanism and revised its placement. In V3, the new position held the pulley assembly more securely than the arrangement used in V2.
To test the V3 intake, the team ran the motors with the intake shafts installed and observed the mechanism operate. During testing, the rotating shafts slipped out of their mounts. This observation established a specific requirement for the next revision: the mounts must limit movement along each shaft’s axis while allowing the shaft to rotate freely. A repeat motor test with the revised mounts would show whether the shafts remain seated during operation.
C. Electronics and Tail Integration
The V3 layout had to accommodate the Raspberry Pi 5, Raspberry Pi Pico W, motor driver, and battery while preserving space for the mechanical assemblies. The team selected a printed circuit board carrier for the Pico W and motor driver as the next step in organizing power distribution and external connections. At the stage covered by the weekly reports, the carrier board was a design decision awaiting implementation.
For the tail, the team retained the first printed tail section for use as the upper portion of the V3 assembly. Mohamed helped print the gear components. During the fit check, the team found that the spacing between two gears prevented them from meshing smoothly. The next verification step was to revise the spacing and check engagement through a full rotation before testing the assembled tail under motor power.
D. Weekly Reports and Project Records
The weekly reports linked each design change to an observation from assembly or testing. Mohamed also updated the team’s GitHub repository with prototype photographs and a component list describing the purpose of each item. His documented contributions included designing the motor housing, assisting with chassis and electronics assembly, driving the prototype during testing, and helping fabricate the tail gears.
By the November 11 update, the team had demonstrated keyboard-controlled driving and motor-driven intake operation. Reliable shaft retention, passage through the hoop, and smooth tail-gear engagement remained open verification items. These observations provide specific checks for the next prototype and a basis for comparing its performance with V3.

